[[SECTION]]
MID: 3e8ec502c79894ea0903b134c95854b7
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-REFERENCE-build-and-release-ownership
TITLE: Build and release ownership

[TEXT]
MID: 58a5191d6e19a7b223d28517681b56dc
STATEMENT: >>>
[LINK: DOC-VERIFICATION-CI-CD, CI/CD architecture] owns verification, artifact certification and release admission. The following describes component artifacts, operational configuration and deployment ownership. A successful component build does not by itself authorize publication.

- UI changes build `app/dist` with `pnpm run build:frontend`, check and test the static Worker, then upload only the `codexia-frontend` asset Worker. An ordinary release runs after the configured base branch advances and uses the `codexia-production` GitHub Environment. The protected R5 initial release uploads the certified assets to an undeployed Worker version and checks that version before promotion. Production UI uses the Full profile. CodexiaCTL owns Zone Routes, including the scriptless exclusions.
- Frontend and Backend use independent SemVer bases in `app/package.json` and `packages/api/package.json`. CI adds the component workflow's run number to the base patch. Compatible changes need no version-only commit; intentionally change the major/minor base for a new version line. Local builds retain the declared base. Successful frontend deployment creates an immutable-by-policy `frontend-v<version>` GitHub Release; certified backend publication creates `backend-v<version>` with Core/Full immutable digest references. Release records include the API epoch. Existing release tags and records may not be repointed or replaced. GitHub workflow run numbers are independent, retries keep the same number, and unused PR numbers are harmless gaps.
- The frontend declares its required API epoch in `deploy/frontend-compatibility.json`. Backend advertises its provided epoch in `deploy/update-compatibility.json` and its running `/api/version` response. Independent release versions are compatible exactly when their API epochs and deployment profiles match. For ordinary releases, require a live compatible backend and the appropriate deployed frontend identity. An absent or incompatible identity stops ordinary release admission. Only the protected R5 initial frontend and R6 Backend-only activation have their explicit clean-install admission. Verify that CodexiaCTL registered and checked the product probe Service Token secrets `CODEXIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID` / `CODEXIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET` for the reviewed HTTPS origin before activation. CI runs the read-only Production Access Preflight in parallel with the build, daily and on manual dispatch from main; it verifies both identity JSON responses, the candidate's required Full profile and API epoch against the live backend, anonymous denial and CI-token denial on non-probe paths. It reports endpoint, HTTP status and CF-Ray without logging credentials or bodies. A Cloudflare `cf-mitigated: challenge` on an identity path is reported separately as `EDGE_CHALLENGE`, not an Access token failure. Anonymous identity probes must reach the authorization boundary; a geographic challenge on an out-of-scope author route proves external denial only. The production mutation job still rechecks live backend compatibility immediately before replacing the UI. The VM checks the actual deployed UI before replacing the backend. The two identity probe paths and their exact Tunnel regexes are defined in `deploy/cloudflare/identity-probes.json`; the exact-path apps admit the product probe under its designated Service Auth policy. The parent application retains author-only authorization. A failing preflight blocks frontend replacement, not repository verification or backend artifact publication. API epoch changes that are incompatible with an installed environment stop without mutation.
- Backend changes build `Dockerfile.backend`, test the resulting image, and publish `ghcr.io/<target-owner>/codexia-backend:sha-<40-character-commit>` (the official deployment uses `yohn-jp`; forks use the verified target owner) only after a push to the configured base branch and successful complete main-push verification for that exact commit, including certification. The publication gate waits up to one hour and fails closed if verification is unavailable or unsuccessful. Core retains the existing `sha-<40-character-commit>` tag. Full is independently built and tested in parallel and published as `ghcr.io/<target-owner>/codexia-backend:sha-<40-character-commit>-full` behind the same exact-source gate. Both images contain only the selected API profile and runtime dependencies, not `app/dist`.
- Release lane selection and the common certification gate are defined in [LINK: DOC-VERIFICATION-CI-CD, CI/CD architecture].
- The production push job in [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT, Push deployment] consumes the exact certified Full digest and provenance certificate after publication. Its protected enable flag stays false until the `codexia` VM account, SSH trust and applicable activation/update checks are accepted. Fresh activation requires no backup destination; ordinary updates require accepted backup and recovery. PR validation never attaches the production Environment or receives deployment SSH credentials. There is no signed pull channel, updater service or timer. Protected push deployment is the sole backend activation path.
- `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` is only available to the protected production Worker job. `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` is a GitHub Environment secret. GHCR publication uses the job-scoped `GITHUB_TOKEN` with `packages: write`.

From the existing trusted SSH session on the target VM, run `codexiactl doctor` or `codexiactl init --plan` and confirm the repository and the public and management hostnames from the certified `deploy/production.json`.
Use `codexiactl init` to review and approve changes to Codexia's DNS records, Tunnel ingress, Access applications and dedicated policies, empty frontend Worker and routes.
The CLI uses the official cf Device Flow and registers the applicable credentials in the protected Environments.
Follow the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ACCESS-POLICY-MIGRATION, Access policy migration] to adopt existing edge resources and handle incomplete inventory or interrupted credential issuance.

The account owner manages the shared Access and identity provider rules.
The zone owner handles any WAF exception for the exact HTTPS identity GET paths.
For `codexia.yohn.jp`, preserve the `OutRage Challenge` rule's non-JP predicate and append `and not (http.host eq "codexia.yohn.jp" and http.request.method eq "GET" and http.request.uri.path in {"/api/version" "/desktop/update-identity.json"})`.
Verify that the exception is restricted to the identity paths from `identity-probes.json` and that the protected preflight still observes the expected Access decisions.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 1d76d16a443ab0fe3950927a4b8a4e5a
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-REFERENCE-vm-environment-and-secrets
TITLE: VM environment and Secrets

[TEXT]
MID: bb79e466ffb79cf6ef2ef02e43c37383
STATEMENT: >>>
[LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-IAC, Infrastructure as code] is the normative VM desired-state authority. The protected Ansible stages inspect the host, converge rootless secrets, storage, network and PostgreSQL, and verify the host connector. The push deployment and encrypted off-VM recovery contracts are defined in [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT, Push deployment] and [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP, Backups].

`deploy/quadlet/codexia-api.container` defines these runtime values:

| Variable | Owner | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `HOST=0.0.0.0` | Container | Listen on the private Podman network. |
| `PORT=3000` | Container | Container API port behind the host publication gate. |
| `CODEXIA_PROFILE=core` | Release | Select the immutable server profile packaged into the image. |
| `CODEXIA_DATA_DIR=/data` | VM operator | Derived module caches; project authoring state lives in PostgreSQL. |
| `CODEXIA_USAGE_DIR=/history` | VM operator | Persistent usage/job history. |
| `CODEXIA_DATABASE_MIGRATE_ON_START=0` | VM operator | Keep schema migration outside service startup; image activation requires a pre-initialized and compatible database. |
| `CODEXIA_DATABASE_PASSWORD_FILE` | Podman secret | Startup reads the hexadecimal `codexia` application-role password and builds the private service connection URL. |
| `CODEXIA_TRUST_PROXY=1` | Origin boundary | Honor HTTPS forwarding within the trusted origin boundary. The published API port binds to host loopback. |

The protected bootstrap stages provision the independent rootless Podman secrets `codexia-postgres-admin-password` and `codexia-postgres-app-password`.
PostgreSQL creates the private `codexia_admin` role and the least-privileged `codexia` application role.
Both secrets are mounted in PostgreSQL for role initialization. The API receives only the application secret.
Ansible installs `deploy/postgres/010-create-app-role.sh` as mode `0555` in the service-owned `0750` directory before the first database start.
The rootless container maps the host service owner to container root, so the PostgreSQL UID needs other-read and execute on this read-only bind.

CodexiaCTL installs the host Tunnel connector and supplies its root-owned systemd credential.
Keep connector credentials and database passwords out of Quadlet source, shell history and logs.
The connector directs management SSH to `127.0.0.1:22` and product ingress to `127.0.0.1:18080`, the listener of `codexia-api.socket`.
The forwarding service `codexia-api-gate.service` connects that listener to the API's loopback-only host port `127.0.0.1:18081`.
The API and PostgreSQL share the internal-only `codexia-data` network. PostgreSQL has no published host port.

The protected Ansible operation sets up systemd lingering and installs the rootless Quadlets.
The `codexia-api.service` dependency starts after `codexia-db.service`, which uses the persistent `codexia-postgres` volume.
Before the first activation, Ansible initializes a pristine application schema during auth staging and verifies the exact ledger again before starting the certified image.
Schema changes to a nonempty database require a separately approved backup, migration, verification and restore procedure.
The API reports ready only while PostgreSQL answers health checks.

The Worker build has no runtime secret or API-origin variable.
The protected production release uses the limited Worker publication token in its GitHub Environment.
Cloudflare Access emails and IdP IDs are configuration, not authentication secrets.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 72f18a8b489e5cfe889a43d52a27b583
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-REFERENCE-health-and-recovery
TITLE: Health and recovery

[TEXT]
MID: 88d6a4d446c9e2a01cfca7b877ca3a61
STATEMENT: >>>
The API exposes unauthenticated process probes at `/health/startup`, `/health/live` and `/health/ready`. The `/api/ready` path also reports request draining.
Quadlet checks `/health/ready` from inside the API container.
From the VM, `curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:2000/ready` checks for an active Cloudflare edge connection.
The operator owns alerting from the loopback-only metrics endpoint.

Deployment, backup and recovery retain the connector and management route while the protected operation closes the publication listener and its forwarding service.
Keep the gate closed when readiness, the running identity or recovery evidence is uncertain.
Follow the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-LIFECYCLE-unified-cloudflare-management, management and publication acceptance] to qualify the closed gate across existing connections, interruption and reboot.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
MID: 56d2d2f00625f7f6eecb032351e36242
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-REFERENCE-persistence-and-backups
TITLE: Persistence and backups

[TEXT]
MID: 4dc5b36de5e0de945d480effcba297ef
STATEMENT: >>>
All workspaces use PostgreSQL as their canonical project, document, revision, Knowledge, and Evidence store. The schema is applied from the API image's existing checksum-verified migrations before the API accepts traffic. Use the explicit migration tooling to import an existing manuscript before opening it. Browser recovery drafts are local to their browser profile and remain separate from the database.

PostgreSQL uses the persistent `codexia-postgres` volume on an internal-only Podman network. Follow the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP, encrypted backup and isolated restore procedures] for the PostgreSQL database and `codexia-history` volume. The protected host transaction quiesces the writer and uses the canonical backup producer under the host lock. The operator retains an independent off-VM copy, verifies recovery on an isolated host, and maintains separate recovery material for database roles, secrets and keys.

The [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP-CONSOLE-OPERATIONS, backup control plane] supports operator-provisioned S3-compatible destinations and the existing encrypted artifacts. The [LINK: DOC-DESIGN-ADMIN-SCREEN-FLOW, owner backup screens] provide configuration, verification, backup requests, and evidence when the control plane is provisioned. Disaster-recovery acceptance requires verified transfer and readback at the actual storage destination and a restore drill on an independent host. [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP-受入シナリオ, Backup acceptance]

The `codexia-data` volume (`/data`) holds derived module caches that can be rebuilt. Retain the prior certified API image and Worker version for protected rollback.

[LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PROTECTED-RECOVERY, Protected recovery handoff] defines the evidence required for the approved R2 restore and R3 publication through the existing deployment path.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: 4527288faac51e985ab011beedced079
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-REFERENCE-rollback-and-live-acceptance
TITLE: Rollback and live acceptance

[TEXT]
MID: 94101b5d21566fa66f23839ea49dd645
STATEMENT: >>>
For a failed Frontend update, the protected Frontend release restores the captured compatible Worker version only after the publication job verifies the deployment and artifact identities.
A conflicting deployment or an unknown current version requires protected recovery.
For a Backend update, follow the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-UPDATE-FLOW, compatible update and rollback] to restore the prior certified digest and verify readiness under the publication gate.
Keep the independent SSH session available until the approved management path is validated.

For live acceptance, verify anonymous Access denial, API login and CSRF protection for writes, an authenticated UI session and API behavior, static asset 404 responses, and Tunnel readiness.
Check that the management SSH route works with the product gate closed and that the protected Frontend rollback restores the expected identity.
Formal operation requires the exact-source certification and integrated service acceptance specified in the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-LIFECYCLE-unified-cloudflare-management, deployment lifecycle].
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
